86045417] [17592186111263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111263 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111263 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/70/702edb8e1ea4a79ea12e891ed3874c36ff3e6628c9cb000753f74ca7bc35ec74.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b35d665-09e4-54b3-8c80-68318024b0ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111263 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a kite. It has a pair of opposite sides that are not parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since the opposite sides of a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a trapezoid. It has only one pair of parallel sides, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $P$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shapes $Q$ and $S$ are not parallelograms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1061 36207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eab37537-55a4-56e5-9fd6-7ee823f295bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1061 9452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04c2712b-f67a-5317-b34e-8936b6acdd9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1061 9454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f398499-a037-5d65-8f25-ecbcfa7534c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1061 9459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14f9869d-2b87-58dd-a3bd-3dfd6047425b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1061 9460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "615b803e-ca7c-56d1-9167-9829ed84b5c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1064 "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](images/94/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1065 2262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06a11fa2-5911-52f5-9a6d-1028e49b44dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1064 "We can organize all mentioned types of quadrilaterals into the following hierarchy diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nEach shape below another is a specific type of the one above it. For example, every square is a rectangle, and every rectangle is a parallelogram. This means every square is also a parallelogram." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1065 5931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d9d3be2f-753b-5a4b-9f26-20b34ab8f89a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186506608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186506644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186506648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1045 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186506608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505900 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0fada08e-9ee8-50a7-a281-27eecc4451e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505914 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505914 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505914 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "015f0057-b143-57ad-8fa5-2978f72ea3dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505925 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505925 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505925 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04cd6f91-4830-5fb2-a760-6d1458397aff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505957 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505957 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505957 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1afb074a-342e-57b7-b473-fffc06924988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506011 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506011 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3cb14bc8-1566-5a66-a92e-6847a87ef3a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506019 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb