b/ref 1079 #uuid "ec71a978-52b7-54a3-b5fa-ef72f160c9d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536811 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536811 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536811 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536811 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b0dd854ae2edb38245466049806c7e93bb4b704a03284905d968869fa1b4146.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the kite above?\n\nI. $AB=AD.$\n\nII. The kite has a pair of congruent opposite angles.\n\nIII. $AB=BC=CD=AD.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13987c3a-59aa-55ed-8fe6-1cb169d96b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536811 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. In the figure, we can see that $AB=AD.$\n\n- Statement II is true. A kite always has a pair of congruent opposite angles.\n\n- Statement III is false. A kite is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. However, not all sides are congruent, for instance, $AB\\ne BC.$\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536834 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536834 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536834 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536834 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4e/4e20dca5a916c43e93dd94b92ff4c1949a111537a8cb2e45b9ca0be7e2300f40.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the square above?\n\nI. $AB=BC=CD=AD.$\n\nII. $m∠ABC=m∠ADC.$\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is parallel to $\\overset{―}{BC}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536834 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55788c3c-c1d0-5ef8-8ff4-5e5fd3b4c618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536834 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A square is a rectangle where all sides are congruent, hence $AB=BC=CD=AD.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The angles in a square are all right. Therefore, $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}=m∠ADC.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Opposite sides of a square are parallel, and $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is opposite $\\overset{―}{BC},$ therefore $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is parallel to $\\overset{―}{BC}.$\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2cfe7fe6-dfad-5186-856c-597f87ad3a5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b24c0db-50fd-51da-9580-556fff1c0632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536948 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536948 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "866cff26-8806-5fb7-acaa-154a2dd5266d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536948 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536811 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
